\begin{abstract}
When the rules of an environment are hidden, an agent has to work them out from what its own actions cause. We ask how much of the advantage of explicit hypothesis testing survives once the information given to each agent is matched. A hypothesis-testing agent, which is given hand-built rule templates, is compared with posterior-sampling and PPO agents that share one macro-action vocabulary, in a procedural grid world with six generalization regimes and in a combination vault. Learned agents without belief inputs trail at difficulty 5 (@{SR('macro_gru',5)!.0%} against @{SR('hypothesis',5)!.0%}, @{NMODELS} training runs) and fail on unseen color symbols (@{TE('macro_gru','D_rule_permutation')!.2f} against @{TE('hypothesis','D_rule_permutation')!.2f}) and on larger vault codes (@{VT('vault_gru','larger')!.2f} against 1.00). A matched-information control, a learned agent that receives the tracker's beliefs, closes most of the gap (@{SR('macro_gruB',5)!.0%} at difficulty 5, @{TE('macro_gruB','D_rule_permutation')!.2f} on unseen colors), so the advantage is largely one of information supplied. A hard exclusivity assumption fails when it is false (@{MK('hypothesis','master_key_d3')!.2f} success); a soft prior that adapts to detectable violations restores @{MK('hyp_adaptive','master_key_d3')!.2f}. With jammed locks, PSRL falls to @{SRN('psrl',3,0.4)!.0%} against @{SRN('hypothesis',3,0.4)!.0%} for the hypothesis agent. On the survival part of CausalGame, where no hypothesis space is supplied, an expected-value-plus-information-gain agent does not beat random search or hill-climbing (@{CGS('Hypothesis (EV + info gain)','survival')!.2f} against @{CGS('Random search','survival')!.2f} and @{CGS('Hill-climbing (reactive)','survival')!.2f}). The environments are synthetic, the vocabularies hand-built and the primitive-action PPO baseline is under-trained.
\end{abstract}
